"Food Smoking House"
"A vast fireplace dominates the westward-lying wall, and when taken into

account, along with a smaller firepit centered in the floor, strongly

suggests this room was used for smoking and preserving meat.  Everything

in the room is stone-crafted, from wall to ceiling to the tables the meat

is supposed to rest on.  Large iron hooks, from which the various sides

of meat were hung during the curing process, are suspended from the

ceiling, some with traces of dried blood and remnants of dead flesh still

attached to them.  The stone tables that ring the chamber bear deep

gouges, ruining their value but not their practical use.  A thick, stone

door designed to retain all possible heat within, blocks passage to the

west.

