"philosopher"
"and he proceeded to relate this story to me:


A caravan of wealthy and stingy men was once plundered in the Pennan

valley and deprived of boundless wealth.  The merchants of the caravan

wept and lamented, beseeching the goddess Algesa to intercede for them

with the robbers, to no avail.  Qe-rohn, the philosopher lately come from

Orlumber, was asked to speak wisdom unto the theives so that they might

return some of the property that was stolen.  Qe-rohn replied: \"It would

be lamentable to utter one word of wisdom to them.  The rust which has

eaten into iron cannot be removed by polishing.  Of what use is preaching

to a black heart?  An iron nail cannot be driven into a rock.  Help the

distressed in your day of propserity because comforting the poor averts

evil from thyself.  When a medicant implores thee for a thing, give it or

else an oppressor may take it by force.\" 

"
E
"journal"
"@cFrom the journal of Thessalie Am'rhani@n


Despite the abundant admonitions of my father to shun musical

entertainments and to prefer solitude and retirement, the budding of my

youth overcame me, my sensual desires were excited so that, unable to

resist them, I walked some steps contrary to the opinion of my father,

enjoying myself in musical amusements and convivial meetings.  When the

advice of my father occurred to my mind, I said \"If the priest was

sitting with us, he would clap his hands.  If the priestess were sipping

wine, she would excuse a drunkard.\" 


Thus I lived til I paid one night a visit to an assembly of people in

which I saw a musician, and it was decided that night that I become a

bard to lead with my harp the llewyrr armies against the men of the town

of Medienne....

